<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/intermediate/impressionism-to-expressionism">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    From Impressionism to Expressionism is <strong>painting\'s response to modernity and the
    camera</strong>: light, then structure, then feeling — each movement a different answer to
    "what is painting FOR now?"
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="outdoors, structure, inner weather, the camera shadow" widget="equationStep" config={impCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the movements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Salon des Refusés (1863):</strong> the rejected painters\' own exhibition — the market lesson\'s disintermediation, art-historical edition: the avant-garde as startup (new distribution channel, new customers, Establishment horrified).</li>
    <li><strong>Japonisme:</strong> Hokusai and Hiroshige in Paris (flat colour, cropped composition — the frame lesson\'s off-centre energy): non-Western influence driving Western modernism, decades before "art beyond the West" entered the curriculum (the next lesson\'s foreshadow).</li>
    <li><strong>Morisot and Cassatt, honestly placed:</strong> core members (not footnotes) — Cassatt\'s mothers-and-children: the gaze reversed, women painting women observed (the theory lesson\'s gaze critique, historically anchored).</li>
    <li><strong>Cézanne as the hinge:</strong> "the father of us all" (Picasso, Matisse) — his doubt-structured apples are the bridge to Cubism; the value lesson\'s planes, about to shatter into the modern lesson.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rewald, The History of Impressionism; the art-history walk-through lesson; the physics lesson\'s colour chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
